# Molecular van der Waals broadening: per-species H2 and He widths with a
# rotational (J) dependence.  Read by src/molecular_broadening.jl.
#
#   gamma_L(J) = max(g0 - dgdJ * J_lower, 0.10 * g0)
#
# in cm^-1 per ATM of the named perturber, as a Lorentz HALF width at half
# maximum, at 296 K, with
#
#   gamma(T) = gamma_L(J) * (296/T)^n
#
# (ExoMol 2024 release paper Sect. 4: P_ref = 1 atm, T_ref = 296 K.  Note ATM,
# not bar.)  J_lower is the LOWER-state total angular momentum.
#
# Korg converts these to its own broadening convention -- the FWHM in angular
# frequency per perturber, summed over H2, He and H I separately -- in
# `molecular_vdW_Gamma`.  See that file's header for the conversion and for the
# differences from ATLAS12/SYNTHE, which uses the same data through a coarser
# kernel.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SOURCES
#
# g0/n:     ExoMol `.broad` files, https://www.exomol.com/db/<SP>/<ISO>/<ISO>__H2.broad
#           (and `__He`), built by atlas12's tools/build_mol_broad.py.  Where a
#           species is J-resolved in ExoMol (H2O, CO, AlH) the tabulated values
#           are collapsed to a median.
#
# dgdJ:     Gharib-Nezhad et al. (2021, ApJS 254, 34; the EXOPLINES database)
#           Table 1, for the five species that paper covers (TiO, VO, CrH, FeH,
#           TiH); their g0 is adopted with it, in preference to ExoMol's
#           rotationally-independent estimate.  The 0.10 floor is their Eq. 5,
#           which exists to stop the linear decline going negative at high J.
#           dgdJ = 0 means no J-dependence is known.
#
#           For the metal OXIDES the two sources agree well -- TiO 0.100 vs
#           ExoMol's 0.0882, ~13% -- so adopting GN+21 changes the J-dependence
#           and not the scale.  For the metal HYDRIDES they do NOT: GN+21 gives
#           all of CrH, FeH and TiH one group value of 0.075, while ExoMol's
#           semi-classical estimates are CrH 0.1858 and TiH 0.2286, a factor of
#           2.5-3.0 higher and in line with its other hydrides (CaH 0.2153,
#           MgH 0.1918, NaH 0.1709).  GN+21 is adopted for all three so that the
#           sibling hydrides stay mutually consistent, but the disagreement is
#           real, unresolved, and larger than anything else in this table.
#
# B:        rotational constant, cm^-1.  Used ONLY to population-weight
#           gamma_L(J) over the thermally populated rotational ladder, since
#           Korg's `Line` carries no per-line J.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PROVENANCE -- READ THIS BEFORE QUOTING ANY OF IT TO PERCENT
#
# ExoMol has EMPIRICAL H2/He broadening for exactly seven molecules (H2O, NH3,
# SO2, CH4, PH3, HCN, H2CO), of which only H2O appears here.  Everything else
# is THEORETICAL, and for the "exotic" pairs -- which is TiO, VO, CaH, CaOH and
# most of this table -- those are simple semi-classical, ROTATIONALLY
# INDEPENDENT estimates from molecular masses and kinetic diameters, with
# n = 0.5 assumed rather than fitted.  That is why most rows carry n exactly
# 0.500.  Gharib-Nezhad et al. note that there is not a single laboratory
# measurement for any of the metal oxides or hydrides here, and their J-slopes
# are themselves assumed by analogy with HCl for large-dipole species.
#
# Treat all of it as good to a factor of a few, not to percent.  It is still an
# enormous improvement on the alternative, which is either no molecular vdW
# broadening at all (Korg's historical behaviour) or the two hardcoded atomic
# constants selected on an electronic-state label (Kurucz's, which is 10-50x
# too large for every electronic band).
#
# Five species have no ExoMol file of their own and take a named chemical
# analogue; the `src` column says which.  (FeH is not one of them despite having
# no ExoMol file -- it takes its numbers from GN+21 directly.)
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DIFFERENCES FROM atlas12's data/mol_broad.dat
#
# 22 species here against SYNTHE's 20.  AlO (Kurucz code 813) and TiH (122) are
# Korg additions: AlO from its own ExoMol .broad file, TiH from GN+21 Table 1.
# NEITHER APPEARS IN SYNTHE'S lines.list, so neither is in the synthe2025 master
# linelist and neither changes any Korg-vs-SYNTHE comparison run on it.  They
# bite only for linelists that actually carry those species -- e.g. one loaded
# straight from ExoMol via `load_ExoMol_linelist`.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# species  g0_H2    n_H2   g0_He    n_He  dgdJ_H2  dgdJ_He      B    src
  CH       0.1076   0.500  0.0600   0.500  0.00000  0.00000  14.190  NH     # first-row hydride
  NH       0.1076   0.500  0.0600   0.500  0.00000  0.00000  16.340  NH
  OH       0.1076   0.500  0.0600   0.500  0.00000  0.00000  18.550  NH     # first-row hydride
  NaH      0.1709   0.500  0.0960   0.500  0.00000  0.00000   4.900  NaH
